      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "That intense, slightly \"screaming\" sound is actually quite common for many modern turbochargers when they are under heavy load, specifically due to the impeller bypassing and air turbulence. While frequency depends on the specific compressor geometry, the scream is often amplified by unburned fuel or oil mist being pushed through the narrow tip clearance, turning into airborne fog that vibrates further. However, this high-pitched whine should not be excessively shrill or accompanied by clattering if the internal bearings and seals are healthy and populated.",
